    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d204">

                <pb facs="0353" n="181" xml:id="pg181"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 841.857/423</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Cork</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Frost</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cork</hi> (<hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Queenstown</hi>)</placeName>, <date
                            ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content-and-sibling-dates"
                            calendar="gregorian" notAfter="1917-03-19T19:00:00-05:00"
                            notBefore="1917-03-18T23:30:00+00:00"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >undated</hi></date>. <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received March
                            19, 1917, 7 p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>British tanker <hi rendition="#italic">Lucilline</hi>, New York bound Calais with
                    naphtha, 2,424 tons register, 35 complement, was struck by torpedo without
                    warning at 2 a.m., March 13, about 40 miles west Bull Light. One man killed by
                    explosion and latter wrecked one lifeboat, necessitating use of small gig which
                    capsized [with?] 12 men, 1 man died fumes naphtha. Survivors saved by admiralty
                    escort which towed <hi rendition="#italic">Lucilline</hi>, bringing her shore
                    two days later. <hi rendition="#italic">Lucilline</hi> had neither wireless nor
                    gun. Made no attempt escape but was under escort. Submarine not seen by <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Lucilline’s</hi> crew but by escort. Weather was
                    moderate sea, light breeze, moonlight alternating black clouds. Sole American,
                    D. W. Townsend, seaman, 1122 Columbia Street, Seattle, survives uninjured. Have
                    joint affidavit Townsend and third officer. This to Ambassador, Consul
                    General.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Frost</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
